WEBVTT

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:05.802
COUNT star answers a very specific question: how many rows are in the current result?

00:00:05.802 --> 00:00:11.507
After a multi-table join, that may not be the same question as how many admissions exist,

00:00:11.507 --> 00:00:15.922
how many lab tests exist, or how many prescriptions exist.

00:00:15.922 --> 00:00:20.592
In our example, the raw join creates eight row combinations even though Alice has only two admissions,

00:00:20.592 --> 00:00:24.222
two labs, and two prescriptions.

00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:27.432
This is why COUNT star can be misleading after joins.

00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:32.272
It counts the combinations produced by the join.

00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:35.982
One safer approach is to count a distinct event identifier, such as COUNT DISTINCT admission ID.

00:00:35.982 --> 00:00:43.872
Another approach is to summarize each child table to the target grain before joining.

00:00:43.872 --> 00:00:47.577
Which approach is better depends on the analytical question.

00:00:47.577 --> 00:00:52.382
Do not add DISTINCT automatically whenever a count looks too large.

00:00:52.382 --> 00:00:55.367
First define the event you are trying to count.

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:58.417
Then choose a method that counts that event correctly.
